Extension API: report a screen ID for the two screens the game's own
dispatcher does not route to.

This is the divergence that was living as a loose file in the orion2re
working tree (`racesel_custom_screen_id.patch`) and therefore in no
list at all. It is here now, generated from the real diff, so it can
be reverse-applied, counted and checked like the other one.

STATUS: needed for HD routing to Select Race and to Custom Race. A
client cannot tell which of the two is on screen without it.

    APPLIED LOCALLY to the orion2re working tree at ~/orion2re
    (engine 1.60.0, git cf4d9617), and it comes back off with

        cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
            ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_screen_id.patch

    ONE FILE, AND IT IS JOES'. `src/game/racesel.cpp` is his, and
    this patch is the whole of what we add to it. All FOUR hunks are
    inside `#ifdef ORION2RE_EXT`, so a build without the flag is his
    code unchanged.

    THAT FILE CARRIES A FOURTH DIVERGENCE WHICH IS NOT IN THIS
    PATCH, deliberately. One unguarded line at racesel.cpp:305,

        _old_race = static_cast<int16_t>(i);

    is the crash fix of `doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md` item 5
    (`racesel.lbx [entry 138]` on Custom Race Accept). It is a
    different subject — a bug in his code, not a feature of ours —
    it is unguarded on purpose, because guarding it would leave the
    crash in a plain build, and it is Joes' to accept or refuse.
    Reverse-applying this patch therefore leaves that one line
    behind, which is correct and is stated here so nobody reports it
    as a failed revert.

    THE HUNKS BELOW ARE GENERATED, and were regenerated on
    5 September 2026 when the fourth was added. They are separated
    from the unguarded point-5 line by removing that single line from
    a scratch copy and diffing against `git archive cf4d9617`; the
    result is applied forward to a pristine export and compared byte
    for byte. It is never hand-edited — an earlier hand-written patch
    in this project would not have applied.

WHY THE IDS ARE MISSING IN THE FIRST PLACE

    `MOX::_current_screen` is the engine's own dispatch variable, not
    a report: `MOX2::Screen_Control_` (mox2.cpp:35) reads it at the
    top of every iteration and switches on it, so the value always
    names the screen the dispatcher will run next. A screen sets it
    when it wants to LEAVE.

    Race selection and Custom Race are not in that switch. Race
    selection is CALLED — from `newgame.cpp:108`, `netstart.cpp:33`
    and `:388`, `hotpop.cpp:150` — and Custom Race
    (`Racial_Option_Screen_`) is called from inside race selection.
    So while either is on screen, `_current_screen` still names
    whoever called them, and an external client sees New Game.

THE FOUR HUNKS

    1. racesel.cpp:212, `Race_Selection_Screen_` entry
       Saves the caller's value and sets `_current_screen =
       SCREEN_RACE` (6).

    2. racesel.cpp:347, the Cancel path of `Race_Selection_Screen_`
       Restores the saved value before `return 0`. ADDED 5 September
       2026; hunk 1 stood alone until then and the section below is
       what it cost.

    3. racesel.cpp:462, `Racial_Option_Screen_` entry
       Saves the current value and sets the synthetic ID 50. Custom
       Race has no ID of its own in `orion2_consts.h`; 50 is ours and
       is documented in `doc/ext_api_dokumentation_v3.md`.

    4. racesel.cpp:642, the Cancel path of `Racial_Option_Screen_`
       Restores the saved value before `return 0`.

    Both Accept paths are deliberately untouched. Every accept from
    race selection goes through `Racial_Option_Screen_`
    (racesel.cpp:308), and that function's accept sets
    `MOX::_current_screen = MOX::_return_screen` (racesel.cpp:692,
    the ORIGINAL's own line) immediately before `finished = 1`. So
    from that assignment on the value already names the destination,
    and a restore of ours would overwrite the game's own intent.

WHAT HUNK 2 IS FOR — the defect it closes, kept because the shape
recurs

    Until 5 September 2026 the entry hunk stood without a restore,
    and that asymmetry was a real defect rather than a tidiness
    question.

    Custom Race is covered on BOTH of its exits, though only one of
    them is ours. Cancel restores through hunk 3. Accept never
    reaches hunk 3 and does not need to: the ORIGINAL's own line
    `MOX::_current_screen = MOX::_return_screen` (racesel.cpp:692)
    runs immediately before `finished = 1`, so from that assignment
    on, the value names the destination. There are exactly two
    returns in that function and both are accounted for.

    Race selection was NOT. `Race_Selection_Screen_` wrote
    `_current_screen` once, on entry, and never again. Its Cancel
    path returned 0 without touching it, and the caller does not
    either: `newgame.cpp:113-119` simply reloads the New Game screen
    and carries on. So after cancelling race selection,
    `_current_screen` reported **SCREEN_RACE (6) while New Game (13)
    was on screen**, and kept reporting it for as long as the player
    stayed there — not for a frame, but until New Game itself left.

    For a client that routes on the screen ID that is an HD Select
    Race drawn over a game showing New Game, with nothing to reveal
    it. Hunk 2 closes it with the same save-and-restore the Custom
    Race pair already used.

    The general shape is worth keeping: **an entry hunk that sets a
    reported value needs an exit hunk on every path the caller does
    not cover.** Custom Race needed one exit because the original
    covered the other; race selection needed one because the original
    covers neither.

